A||||4||BPacholka||Coolant Tubes||||1||
Z||000000||BPacholka||09-27-2001||08:51 AM||||The brackets holding the coolant tube under the drivers side of my 85 GT broke and allowed the tube to hit something and kink up. I have checked with my local junkyards and cant find any within 50 miles that have any Fieros. Does anyone have any fixes for this. Can I cut out the kink and use rubber hose as a splice? Does anyone sell aftermarket tubes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-27-2001||08:58 AM||||Rodney Dickman offers a replacement crossover tube - stainless, I believe.  Less than $100 last time I checked.<P>I think the LHS of the coolant crossover normally attaches to the transaxle with one bolt; you could probably cobble together some kind of restraint with a big "P" clamp, or make something from a spare hose clamp and a homemade bracket.<P>...and then there's bailing wire, duct tape, etc.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Even Stu wouldn't stoop that low, except to get by in a pinch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||09-27-2001||10:22 AM||||You might be able to cut out the damaged section and splice in a piece of hose. Not too pretty, but I've heard of it being done.<BR>AFAIK, no one sells aftermarket tubes to replace the ones under the doors.<BR>Kick Hill Farm <A HREF="http://www.kickhill.com/fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kickhill.com/fiero.html</A>  probably has a used one.<BR>Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top</A>  probably does, too.<P>You might also try posting in the mall with your needs. <BR>I understand that they are not fun to replace.<P>Welcome to the Forum!!<BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT-maniac||09-28-2001||04:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BPacholka:<BR><B>The brackets holding the coolant tube under the drivers side of my 85 GT broke and allowed the tube to hit something and kink up. I have checked with my local junkyards and cant find any within 50 miles that have any Fieros. Does anyone have any fixes for this. Can I cut out the kink and use rubber hose as a splice? Does anyone sell aftermarket tubes?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the damaged part is close to the back you can cut it out and replace it with a longer piece of hose which is what I did. Seems to work fine, haven't had any leaks yet. You can also hold the coolant pipes up by screwing a hose clamp to the body and holding it up with that. It's not pretty but will do the job if your original clamps are rusted away. You can still buy the clamps from GM BTW.<BR>If you can get a good set of pipes from a wrecker be aware that the pipes have different ends depending on which engine was in the car. They're a bugger to get out too. I had to drop the front suspension to get mine in. <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero005a.jpg"> <P>85 WS6 GT partly modified<BR>87 GT Loaded resto project||||reg||1
Z||000004||roadwarrior||09-28-2001||07:00 PM||||I've got a set of used ones if you want, just pay the shipping and they are yours<P>||||reg||1
